-                <!-- 0 — Tree intro: religious / pre-scientific forms (tree on right, copy left) -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="0" data-layout="tree-overlay">
-                    <div class="tree-panel">
-                    <div class="title">Meditation as a spiritual practice</div>
-                    Across cultures, each religion has distinct contemplative practices:
-                    <b>Dhikr</b> in Islam, <b>Lectio Divina</b> in Christianity, <b>Hitbodedut</b> in Judaism,
-                    <b>Vipassanā</b> in the Pāli Buddhist tradition<sup><a class="cite" href="#sources">2</a></sup>,
-                    <b>Prāṇāyāma</b> in Hinduism, and <b>Neidan</b> in Daoism.
-                    <br /><br />
-                    Meditation practices are diverse and widespread but there is a common root: quieting the mind and focusing attention. 
-                    It wasn't until meditative practices spread throughout North America in the 20th century that Western Medicine became 
-                    interested in studying its potential palliative benefits.
-                    </div>
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 1 — Tree intro: science-based crown (red circle highlights the top) -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="1" data-layout="tree-overlay">
-                    <div class="tree-panel">
-                    <div class="title">Meditation as an object of study</div>
-                    <b>Mindfulness</b> became the most popular North American practice by the mid 20th century. It was adapted from the Theravāda Buddhist tradition
-                    and developed into two practices for stress relief and mental health. <br><br> Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) and Mindfulness-Based Cognitive
-                    Therapy (MBCT) are attributed to the work of Howard Kabat-Zinn, a medical researcher at the UMass Center for Mindfulness in the late 1970's.
-                    <br><br>The program is simple, but challenging: Observe your breath. Be present to your expereince without judging it.
-                    </div>
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 2 — Meditation-Based Studies by condition (single color) -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="2">
-                    <div class="title">An Overview of Clincal Trials on Meditaiton</div>
-                    This chart sumarizes over 2,000 clinical trials using mediation as an intervention on various conditions.
-                    Much research has been conducted on psychological and emotional conditions: Stress, Burnout and Depression.
-                    Studies on physiological diseases like Cardiovascular conditions, Neurological/Cognitive Disorders and Anxiety
-                    are much less numerous.
-                    <br>
-                    <a class="sources-link" href="#sources">View all sources →</a>
-                    <br>
-                    We want to better understand what actually happens in our bodies that makes meditation/mindfulness part of a healthy lifestyle.
-                    
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 3 — Bridge (full text) -->
-                <section class="step bridge" data-active-index="3" data-layout="full-text">
-                    <div class="title">How Meditation affects our bodies</div>
-                    Meditation is about focusing on the breath. Our respiratory system delivers oxygen throughout the body in our blood.
-                    Science tells us that deep breathing regulates our nervous system and heart rate. We can use movement meditation to elevate our
-                    heart rate, like Yoga. Or we can use a different movement meditation, Chi Gong, to lower it. Both movement based practices target
-                    our respiration and heart rate, but in opposite directions.
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 4 — Heart-rate STORY CAROUSEL (HTML / Chart.js, no p5 viz) -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="4" data-layout="full-text">
-                    <div class="title">What can a heartbeat tell us about meditation?</div>
-                    <div class="hr-carousel" id="hrCarousel">
-                        <div class="hr-tabs" id="hrTabs" role="tablist"></div>
