-                        <p class="hr-hint">Rotates every 3s · hover to focus · click a tab or dot to hold a story</p>
-                    </div>
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 5 — Bridge (full text) -->
-                <section class="step bridge" data-active-index="5" data-layout="full-text">
-                    <div class="title">The Holistic Health Picture</div> 
-                    <ol>The four pillars of health are:
-                    <li>Sleep</li>
-                    <li>Nutrition</li>
-                    <li>Exercise</li>
-                    <li>Stress</li>
-                </ol>
-                To maximize our health outcomes, we have to find ways to regularly meet all of these needs. <i>How does meditation fit into these health pillars?</i>
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 6 — Stress x sleep scatter -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="6">
-                    <div class="title">Stress and Sleep</div>
-                    This study looked at 374 individuals and found that stress and sleep quality are strongly, negatively linked
-                    (r = −0.90). Even people without insomnia or sleep apnea reported sleeping worse as their stress increased. <br><br> You can imagine how this moves in a cycle:
-                    The higher stress you feel, your sleep quality gets lower, your body can't recover in sleep, and the more stress you feel.
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 7 — Stress tiers -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="7">
-                    <div class="title">High stress, worse on every axis</div>
-                    This chart aggregates all the participants from the previously mentioned study and summarizes their findings: low-stress
-                    individuals average 8.3 sleep quality and 67 BPM resting heart rate; high-stress
-                    individuals fall to 5.9 and rise to 74 BPM.<br><br>
-                    Stress has a negative effect on sleep. If we want to sleep better, we have to find ways to lower our stress!
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 8 — Means Bar Chart -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="8">
-                    <div class="title">Meditation lowers reported stress after a single session</div>
-                    Alessandro Sparacio and several collaborators ran a 37 site study with over 2,200 participants to study how meditation
-                    affects how people report their levels of stress. The control group listened to a chapter from an audio book while the other groups practiced a guided
-                    meditation in one of three therapeutic meditation practices.<br><br> 
-                    “All exercises proved to be more efficacious than the active control. We observed a mean difference 
-                    of 0.27 (d = −0.56; 95% CI −0.43 to −0.69) between the control condition (M = 1.95, SD = 0.50) 
-                    and the condition with the largest stress reduction (body scan: M = 1.68, SD = 0.46).<br>
-Our findings suggest that mindfulness may be beneficial for reducing self-reported short-term stress for English speakers from higher-income countries.” 
-<a href = https://osf.io/6w2zm/wiki?wiki=g7zf4>Self-Administered Mindfulness Interventions Reduce Stress in a Large, Randomized Controlled Multi-Site Study.</a>
-                </section>
-
