-                    The participants were randomly organized into different groups, one for control and the others into different
-                    meditative exercises. They completed the exercises once and were asked to report their current stress levels
-                    to their stress before the experiment. While 17% of the control group reported to have reduce stress into the lowest range (20-29),
-                    35% of body scan group reported in the lowest range. This shows that 
-
-                </section>
-
-                <!-- 10 — Conclusion (full text) -->
-                <section class="step" data-active-index="10" data-layout="full-text">
-                    <div class="title">The honest conclusion</div>
-                    The data supports a measured claim, not a miracle. Meditation has strong, replicated benefits for
-                    attention and for the stress mechanisms that erode sleep. But within-session physiological effects like heart
-                    rate are inconsistent and technique-dependent. Meditation works best as one practice among
-                    several — paired with sleep hygiene, activity, and care — not as a standalone cure.
-                </section>
