+<body>
+    <canvas id="motes"></canvas>
+
+    <!-- OPENING TITLE CARD: full-screen hero; fades out as the user scrolls into the tree -->
+    <div id="hero">
+        <div id="hero-inner">
+            <h1 id="hero-title">Is mindfulness an effective treatment toward a healthier lifestyle?</h1>
+            <div id="hero-authors">Franck Kepnang &amp; Aaron Stern</div>
+            <div id="hero-scroll">scroll to begin</div>
+        </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <header id="site-header">
+        <span id="site-title">Is mindfulness an effective treatment?</span>
+        <span id="site-course">IMT 561 Data Visualization: Design and Development</span>
+        <span id="debug-state"></span>
+    </header>
+    <div class="container">
+        <div id='graphic'>
+            <div id='sections'>
+
+                <!-- spacer: gives the opening hero room to fade before the tree -->
+                <div id="hero-spacer" style="height: 62vh;"></div>
+
+                <!-- 0 — Tree intro: religious / pre-scientific forms (tree on right, copy left) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="0" data-layout="tree-overlay">
+                    <div class="tree-panel">
+                    <div class="title">Meditation is older than medicine</div>
+                    Long before it entered the clinic, meditation was a devotional discipline. The word itself
+                    descends from the Latin <i>meditārī</i> — "to think over, to reflect upon"<sup><a class="cite"
+                    href="#sources">1</a></sup>. Across cultures, distinct contemplative forms took root:
+                    <b>Dhikr</b> in Islam, <b>Lectio Divina</b> in Christianity, <b>Hitbodedut</b> in Judaism,
+                    <b>Vipassanā</b> in the Pāli Buddhist tradition<sup><a class="cite" href="#sources">2</a></sup>,
+                    <b>Prāṇāyāma</b> in Hinduism, and <b>Neidan</b> in Daoism.
+                    <br /><br />
+                    These practices share a single root — the deliberate turning of attention inward — but their
+                    purpose was spiritual, not therapeutic. None were designed to be measured. Scroll to grow the
+                    tree from these roots, then climb toward its modern crown.
+                    </div>
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 1 — Tree intro: science-based crown (red circle highlights the top) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="1" data-layout="tree-overlay">
+                    <div class="tree-panel">
+                    <div class="title">Then science began to ask</div>
+                    Beginning in the late twentieth century, clinicians took the oldest of these techniques and
+                    subjected it to measurement. <b>Mindfulness</b> — adapted from the Theravāda Buddhist tradition
+                    and standardized as Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) and Mindfulness-Based Cognitive
+                    Therapy (MBCT) at the UMass Center for Mindfulness — became the most-studied form<sup><a
+                    class="cite" href="#sources">3</a></sup>. Its core instruction is deceptively simple: observe
+                    your thoughts without attaching judgment.
+                    <br /><br />
+                    At the crown of the tree, circled in red, the question itself changes. No longer <i>is this
+                    sacred?</i> but <i>does it work, and how would we know?</i>
+                    <br /><br />
+                    To find out, we looked at the evidence three ways: 2,411 clinical trials, physiological heart
+                    rate data recorded during meditation sessions, and health surveys from 374 participants.
+                    <br /><br />
+                    <a class="sources-link" href="#sources">View all sources →</a>
+                    </div>
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 2 — Meditation-Based Studies by condition (single color) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="2">
+                    <div class="title">What is meditation studied for?</div>
+                    To map where clinical attention has concentrated, we aggregated 2,411 meditation trials
+                    registered on ClinicalTrials.gov<sup><a class="cite" href="#sources">B</a></sup> and grouped them
+                    by primary condition, combining mindfulness and other meditation traditions into a single
+                    category. Each bar reports the total number of registered trials for that condition.
+                    <br /><br />
+                    The distribution is concentrated in psychological and stress-related conditions:
+                    <b>chronic pain</b>, <b>stress and burnout</b>, and <b>depression</b> account for the largest
+                    share of trials, while <b>anxiety</b> and <b>neurological or cognitive</b> conditions are studied
+                    far less often. This reflects where meditation has been positioned as a candidate intervention,
+                    not where it has been proven effective. Trial volume measures research interest; it does not, on
+                    its own, establish clinical benefit.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 3 — Bridge (full text) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="3" data-layout="full-text">
+                    <div class="title">But volume isn't proof</div>
+                    Thousands of trials tell us meditation is <i>studied</i> — not that it <i>works</i>. To judge that,
+                    we look inside the body. We start with a classic physiological question, and the data turns out to
+                    tell more than one story.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 4 — Heart-rate STORY CAROUSEL (HTML / Chart.js, no p5 viz) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="4" data-layout="full-text">
+                    <div class="hr-scrolly">
+                        <div class="hr-graphic">
+                            <div class="two-hearts" id="hrTwoHearts"></div>
+                        </div>
+                        <div class="hr-story">
+                            <div class="title">What can a heartbeat tell us about meditation?</div>
+                            <p>Before anyone meditates, the two hearts are simply at rest &mdash; and already very different. The chi practitioner sits near <b>81</b> beats per minute; the kundalini practitioner is much calmer, around <b>61</b>. Steady and predictable, nothing dramatic.</p>
+                            <div class="hr-switch">
+                                <button id="hrToDuring" type="button">&#9654;&nbsp; Start the meditation</button>
+                            </div>
+                            <p>Now they meditate &mdash; and head in opposite directions. The chi practitioner&rsquo;s heart eases down a little. The kundalini practitioner&rsquo;s races <i>upward</i>, past <b>90</b>, driven by fast, forceful breathing. Same word, &ldquo;meditation,&rdquo; two opposite effects on the body.</p>
+                            <p>So meditation isn&rsquo;t one thing. The quiet, mindful kind can gently lower your heart rate; the active kind winds it up. Both are useful for the right goal &mdash; which is why mindfulness works best as a <b>complement</b> to a healthy routine, not a cure on its own.</p>
+                            <p class="hr-limit"><b>One caveat:</b> this is a small study &mdash; 8 chi and 4 kundalini practitioners &mdash; and each heart here is a single person. It shows the pattern clearly, but treat the exact numbers as a hint, not proof.</p>
+                            <p class="hr-cite">Source: Peng et&nbsp;al., &ldquo;Exaggerated Heart Rate Oscillations During Two Meditation Techniques,&rdquo; <i>Int. J. Cardiol.</i> (1999). See <a class="sources-link" href="#sources">Sources</a> for full references.</p>
+                        </div>
+                    </div>
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 5 — Bridge (full text) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="5" data-layout="full-text">
+                    <div class="title">So where does meditation clearly help?</div>
+                    The physiological picture is mixed. The stronger case lives one step upstream — in <b>stress</b>,
+                    and the damage chronic stress does to sleep. This is the mechanism meditation is best positioned to
+                    interrupt.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 6 — Stress x sleep scatter -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="6">
+                    <div class="title">Stress and sleep move together</div>
+                    Across 374 individuals, stress and sleep quality are strongly, negatively linked
+                    (<b>r = −0.90</b>). As reported stress climbs, sleep quality collapses. Color marks who carries a
+                    diagnosed sleep disorder — they cluster exactly where you'd expect.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 7 — Stress tiers -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="7">
+                    <div class="title">High stress, worse on every axis</div>
+                    Group people into low, mid, and high stress and the pattern holds across metrics: low-stress
+                    individuals average <b>8.3</b> sleep quality and <b>67 BPM</b> resting heart rate; high-stress
+                    individuals fall to <b>5.9</b> and rise to <b>74 BPM</b>. Each bar is scaled within its own range
+                    so the comparison is honest.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 8 — Conclusion (full text) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="8" data-layout="full-text">
+                    <div class="title">The honest conclusion</div>
+                    The data supports a measured claim, not a miracle. Meditation has strong, replicated benefits for
+                    <b>attention</b> and for the <b>stress mechanisms</b> that erode sleep — expert meditators show far
+                    less mind-wandering (0.32 vs 0.40 in novices). But within-session physiological effects like heart
+                    rate are inconsistent and technique-dependent. Meditation works best as <b>one practice among
+                    several</b> — paired with sleep hygiene, activity, and care — not as a standalone cure.
+                </section>
+
+                <!-- 9 — Sources (full text) -->
+                <section class="step" data-active-index="9" data-layout="full-text" id="sources">
+                    <div class="title">Sources</div>
+                    <div style="font-size:0.95em; line-height:1.8;">
+                        <b>Definitions &amp; background</b><br />
+                        1. <i>Oxford English Dictionary</i>, "meditate." Oxford University Press.
+                        en.oxforddictionaries.com/definition/meditate<br />
+                        2. University of Oxford, Faculty of Asian and Middle Eastern Studies — Pāli &amp; the
+                        Theravāda tradition. ames.ox.ac.uk/pali<br />
+                        3. UMass Memorial Health — Center for Mindfulness (MBSR / MBCT; Jon Kabat-Zinn).
+                        ummhealth.org/services-treatments/center-mindfulness<br /><br />
+                        <b>Data</b><br />
+                        A. OpenNeuro ds001787 — EEG meditation study (expert vs. novice).<br />
+                        B. ClinicalTrials.gov — meditation trials by condition.<br />
